# Force LF line endings on all text files so files we hash at build/test
# time have a stable byte representation across platforms. Without this,
# GitHub Actions Windows runners with the default `core.autocrlf=true`
# mutate `\n` → `\r\n` on checkout, which silently changes file content
# and breaks any test that pins an embedded fixture's SHA-256.
#
# The canonical example is `internal/api/data/openapi.json`: its digest
# is pinned at compile time (`api.EmbeddedSpecSHA256`) and asserted in
# `registry_test.go`. CRLF normalisation produces a different file, which
# fails the test on Windows and ships a different byte stream than every
# other platform's binary.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Pin canonical text formats explicitly so a future change to git's auto
# detection cannot drift them.
*.json   text eol=lf
*.yaml   text eol=lf
*.yml    text eol=lf
*.toml   text eol=lf
*.md     text eol=lf
*.go     text eol=lf
*.sh     text eol=lf
*.py     text eol=lf

# Binary file types — `core.autocrlf` must not touch these. The release
# artefact zips are the primary motivation; the others are defensive.
*.zip    binary
*.tar    binary
*.tar.gz binary
*.tgz    binary
*.gz     binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
